# component: select
# decisions: API-19, API-24, API-26, BRD-1, DOC-1, DOC-2, FOC-1, FOC-3, FOC-4, FOC-5, FOC-6, FRM-4, INT-1, OVL-13
# hunks:
#   API-19: `SelectItem` calls Item's `useItemDescriptionId()`, provides its `register` through
#     `ItemDescriptionContext`, and sets `aria-describedby` on the option once an `ItemDescription`
#     inside it registers, so a two-line option (`ItemContent` > `ItemTitle` + `ItemDescription`)
#     reads its second line as the option's accessible description. A one-line option links nothing.
#   FOC-1, FOC-3, FOC-4, FOC-6: the trigger's `outline-none focus-visible:border-ring
#     focus-visible:ring-3 focus-visible:ring-ring/50` becomes `focus:border-ring/70` — the border
#     tint on `:focus`, for mouse and keyboard alike — and the dropped `outline-none` lets the
#     global `:focus-visible` outline through. A select trigger is a BUTTON, so it keeps the
#     outline (FOC-4); Input and Textarea suppress theirs (FOC-3).
#   FOC-5: the invalid tint becomes `not-focus:aria-invalid:border-destructive` (and its dark
#     counterpart), so focus outranks it; the `aria-invalid` halo rings go with the rest.
#   INT-1: `cursor-default` is removed from `SelectItem`, so a row shows the hand cursor the one
#     global rule gives every `[role="option"]`. The two scroll arrows KEEP theirs — `base.css`
#     names them as a sanctioned non-pointer cursor.
#   FRM-4: `data-disabled:pointer-events-none` is dropped from `SelectItem`, so a disabled row
#     stays hoverable and a Tooltip can explain it.
#   OVL-13: `SelectContent` reads `useInternalThemeScope()` and attaches it to the Positioner
#     inside the Portal, so a nested theme scope survives the portal boundary.
#     `tooling/verify-portal-theme-scope.mjs` inventories this host.
#   BRD-1: `SelectContent` draws a real 1px `border border-border` instead of upstream's
#     `ring-1 ring-foreground/10` box-shadow outline (MK reversed BRD-1 to ours, 23-09-2026). A
#     border survives forced colours and matches `foundations/elevation`; nothing else moves.
#   API-24: `SelectTrigger` gains `variant: "outline" | "ghost"` (default `outline`, reflected as
#     `data-variant`), written as a CVA axis so a consumer width or border class still wins. The
#     default trigger is `w-full` instead of upstream's `w-fit`, so a Select takes its width from
#     its parent like Input, Combobox and every other form control; `ghost` keeps `w-fit`, drops
#     the border (and the dark-mode fill) at rest, and shows the border on hover, on `:focus` (the
#     FOC-3 tint) and while the popup is open — the inline tier for table rows and toolbars. An
#     outline trigger with no width of its own takes `[[data-slot=button-group]>&]:w-fit` — as a
#     ButtonGroup's direct child, exactly the scope of upstream's group rule — because upstream's
#     ButtonGroup sizes an unsized trigger to its content with a `:not([class*='w-'])` rule the
#     `w-full` default would otherwise defeat.
#   API-26: NO HUNK for the Field wiring — Base UI's `Select.Root` and `Select.Trigger` read the
#     `Field` context themselves (label, description and error ids, `aria-invalid`);
#     `select.test.tsx` pins it.
#   cn: `import { cn } from "cn"` becomes `@vegastack/design` — our npm layer owns `cn` (DOC-2).
#   formatting: the repository formatter (prettier defaults: semicolons, trailing commas)
#               reflows the file, and `registry-stamp.mjs` writes the provenance header the
#               three-copy model needs (DOC-1); those hunks carry no decision of their own.
